<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Yongli Emperor</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1623">1623</a>–<a href="/years/1662">1662</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Also known as Zhu Youlang · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/southern-ming">Southern Ming</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Yongli Emperor, Zhu Youlang, was the last emperor of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/southern-ming">Southern Ming</a>, enthroned at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhaoqing">Zhaoqing</a> in 1646. Backed for a time by former rebel armies under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/li-dingguo">Li Dingguo</a>, he held parts of southern and south-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> against the Qing, then fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burma">Burma</a> in 1659. The Burmese handed him over to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wu-sangui">Wu Sangui</a>, who had him executed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kunming">Kunming</a> in 1662.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Zhu Youlang was the son of Zhu Changying, the seventh son of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wanli-emperor">Wanli Emperor</a>, and of Empress Dowager Ma. He inherited the title Prince of Gui from his brother and lived obscurely as a minor member of the imperial family until the peasant rebel <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/li-zicheng">Li Zicheng</a> took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a> in 1644 and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chongzhen-emperor">Chongzhen Emperor</a> killed himself at Coal Hill. Six weeks later the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manchus">Manchu</a> army entered the city and proclaimed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qing-empire">Qing dynasty</a>, and over the next two years the loyalist Hongguang, Longwu and Shaowu emperors were captured and executed in quick succession.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Zhu Youlang became Caretaker of the State on 20 November 1646, after the death of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/longwu-emperor">Longwu Emperor</a>. When Longwu's brother proclaimed himself emperor as Shaowu, Youlang took the throne on 24 December 1646 with the era name Yongli, meaning perpetual calendar; their brief civil war ended a month later when the Qing captured and executed Shaowu. For his first five years his adoptive mother, Empress Dowager Wang, acted as his adviser, and it was said that he followed her wishes in everything.</p><p>Qing pressure drove the court from Guilin in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guangxi">Guangxi</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jiangxi">Jiangxi</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hunan">Hunan</a>, then to Nanning. Youlang came to rely on local warlords and bandit chieftains, the most effective of whom, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/li-dingguo">Li Dingguo</a>, enlarged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/southern-ming">Southern Ming</a> territory in the south-west for five years. The Qing answered by placing the region under the former Ming official Hong Chengchou as governor-general of five provinces, and by 1658 Youlang had been forced back into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yunnan">Yunnan</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pindale-min">Pindale Min</a>, king of the Taungoo dynasty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burma">Burma</a>, allowed him to live at Sagaing, near Ava, if his men gave up their weapons. Fighting soon broke out between the exiles and their hosts; after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pye-min">Pye Min</a> overthrew Pindale in May 1661, he ordered the Chinese followers killed, sparing only Youlang. In December 1661 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wu-sangui">Wu Sangui</a> entered Burma with 20,000 men and demanded the emperor, and on 22 January 1662 he and his sons and grandsons were sent by boat to Wu's camp. Taken to Yunnanfu, he was strangled by Wu Sangui in June. Zhu Shugui, Prince of Ningjing, performed rites in his name in the Kingdom of Tungning until 1683.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Yongli Emperor” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Between 1658 and 1662 Ming loyalist forces, following the fugitive <b>Yongli Emperor</b> into Burma, raided the country, and in 1662 a Qing army under Wu Sangui reached Ava, withdrawing only after the Burmese court handed over the emperor.</blockquote><a href="/empire/restored-toungoo-kingdom">Restored Toungoo Kingdom · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Nanjing fell in 1645 after the massacre at Yangzhou, the Longwu Emperor, enthroned at Fuzhou, was captured in Fujian in 1646, and the <b>Yongli Emperor</b> ruled from Zhaoqing and later the south-west.</blockquote><a href="/state/southern-ming">Southern Ming</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/china">China</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kunming">Kunming</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/china">China</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=39.9,116.41&amp;year=1648" aria-label="Beijing on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7418 736 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Yongli Emperor"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7868 1006h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7868 1006h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=39.9,116.41&amp;year=1648">What was here in 1648? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
